[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Authentication and Authorization Strategies in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing a GraphQL API requires a different mindset compared to traditional REST APIs. Because GraphQL uses a single endpoint for all operations and clients can request arbitrary shapes of data, security must be handled gracefully at both the transport layer and the resolver level."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Authentication vs. Authorization"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authentication is the process of verifying *who* the user is, typically handled via JWT (JSON Web Tokens), session cookies, or API keys passed in the HTTP headers."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authorization is the process of determining *what* the authenticated user is allowed to do, handled within resolvers, schema directives, or middleware."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Context and Authentication Middleware"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The standard approach to authentication in GraphQL servers (like Apollo Server or Express-GraphQL) is to extract credentials from the incoming HTTP request and attach the user object to the context. This context is then made available to every resolver."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"const { ApolloServer } = require('apollo-server');

const server = new ApolloServer({
  typeDefs,
  resolvers,
  context: ({ req }) => {
    const token = req.headers.authorization || '';
    const user = getUserFromToken(token);
    return { user };
  },
});"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Implementing Authorization in Resolvers"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the user is present in the context, you can protect individual resolvers by checking permissions imperatively. If the user lacks the required role, throw an authentication or forbidden error."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    adminData: (parent, args, context) => {
      if (!context.user || context.user.role !== 'ADMIN') {
        throw new Error('Access denied! Insufficient permissions.');
      }
      return fetchAdminData();
    },
  },
};"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Schema Directives for Declarative Authorization"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"For cleaner and more declarative code, you can use custom schema directives. This allows you to apply security constraints directly inside the GraphQL SDL (Schema Definition Language)."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"directive @auth(requires: Role = ADMIN) on FIELD_DEFINITION

enum Role {
  ADMIN
  USER
}

type Query {
  secretContent: String @auth(requires: USER)
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Best Practices for Production"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always validate tokens at the gateway or context creation step, avoiding repetitive parsing logic inside resolvers."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement query complexity and depth limiting to prevent malicious actors from executing deeply nested, unauthorized resource queries that cause Denial of Service (DoS)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle field-level authorization carefully to ensure unauthorized users do not receive sensitive scalar fields requested alongside public data."}]
